# Clean Code — Comments

The proper use of comments is to compensate for the failure to express intent in code. Code should be the primary source of truth.

## CMT-01 — Explain yourself in code first

Before writing a comment, try to make the code self-evident through better naming or structure. A well-named function eliminates the need for most explanatory comments.

```
// bad
// Check whether the employee is eligible for full benefits
if employee.flags & HOURLY_FLAG && employee.age > 65

// good
if employee.isEligibleForFullBenefits()
```

## CMT-02 — Do not be redundant

A comment that restates exactly what the code says adds noise and must be maintained alongside the code it duplicates.

```
// bad
// Returns the day of the month
function getDayOfMonth()
  return dayOfMonth  // return the day of month
end function
```

## CMT-03 — Do not add obvious noise

Mandate comments on every field, getter, or setter communicate nothing and train readers to ignore all comments.

```
// bad — restates the obvious; reader learns nothing
// The name of the user
name = ""

// Returns the user's name
function getName()
  return name
end function

// good — no comment needed; the name is self-evident
name = ""

function getName()
  return name
end function
```

## CMT-04 — Do not use closing-brace comments

If you feel the need to mark the end of a block, the block is too long. Shorten the function instead.

```
// bad
while condition
  // ... many lines ...
end while  // while condition
```

## CMT-05 — Never comment out code

Commented-out code becomes dead weight. Readers assume it is meaningful and dare not delete it. Use version control — if the code might be needed, the history preserves it.

## CMT-06 — Use comments to explain intent

A comment earns its place when it explains *why* a decision was made — something the code cannot express on its own.

```
// We sort in reverse order here because downstream consumers
// expect the most-recent entry first and cannot be changed.
results.sort(reverseChronological)
```

## CMT-07 — Use comments to clarify obscure code

When calling an external API or working around a known language quirk that cannot be renamed or restructured, a short clarification comment is appropriate.

## CMT-08 — Use comments to warn of consequences

Alert other developers to non-obvious risks: performance costs, non-thread-safe code, destructive operations, or environment constraints.

```
// WARNING: this operation is irreversible and will delete all
// customer records in the target environment. Do not run in production.
```

## CMT-09 — Mark unfinished work with TODO or FIXME

Tag incomplete or known-broken code so it can be searched and tracked. Do not leave silent gaps.

- `TODO:` — work that must be done before the feature is complete.
- `FIXME:` — a known issue that should be fixed but is not blocking.

```
// TODO: replace with paginated query once dataset exceeds 10k rows
results = db.fetchAll(query)

// FIXME: this calculation is wrong for leap years
daysRemaining = DAYS_IN_YEAR - currentDay
```
